From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hunter B Hough <git@hough.ink>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 17:09:31 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 163/166] fix(gastown): stop promising a session_sleep restart
 gc forbids on the IDLE exits (gcp-hnhg)

The deacon, witness and refinery patrol formulas end an idle turn with
"The session_sleep policy will restart this session after the configured
idle interval". gc rejects a sleep policy on a mode="always" named session at
config load (internal/config/config.go, 'named_session %q: mode %q is
incompatible with sleep_after_idle'), and the deacon and witness are
mode="always" in pack.toml, so no city or rig session_sleep value can ever
cover them. The refinery is mode="on_demand" but sets no sleep_after_idle
either. Agents followed the text, ended their turn with IDLE, and sat
silent for 1.2-5.5h; gcp-m7dn was merge-ready for ~1.5h behind one such stall.

This is fix (1) of the bead, prose only. Each IDLE exit now says nothing
restarts the session on an idle interval and names the mechanism that does:
the controller's idle_timeout stop (deacon/witness 1h, refinery 2h) followed
by an immediate restart for the always-mode roles, or a restart on routed
work for the on_demand refinery. The deferral gc applies while a session
holds assigned work is stated for the always-mode roles. Every nudge clause
is struck. The matching comment in witness-heartbeat-check.sh is corrected;
STALE_MIN and its arithmetic are unchanged. The existing pin that the deacon
formula keeps 'the restarted session resumes from it' still holds.

test_idle_exit_promises_no_session_sleep_restart pins both halves: no
'session_sleep policy (will restart|restarts)' anywhere in the pack outside
tests, and each IDLE exit carries the corrected wording exactly once. It
fails on the old text with 4 hits.

Not changed, by design: pack.toml modes, agents/, idle_timeout, any
[session_sleep] block (a hard config-load error), and a real resume path for
always-mode roles (fix (2), not filed per the seat ruling).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
